[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Chris sitting on a bench in a field, introducing the problem of static self-filmed shots.
AUDIO: 'Now, you can tell when somebody film themselves and it\'s not the camera they shot it on, it\'s they\'re the same size in every single shot. The same distance, the same framing'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video after video and there\'s simply nobody there to change it. I\'ve been a TV cameraman for 25 years and I want to show you how you can make it feel like you have your own personal camera'

[00:25] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Chris standing in a field, demonstrating a subtle zoom.
AUDIO: 'operator on your own using your phone. Basically, we\'re going to cheat. So, what is that person actually doing? Well, I\'ll give you'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an example that sticks with me. I was on the Queen\'s funeral for the BBC, Kirsty Young\'s final piece to camera and as you\'re speaking, I just eased in very slightly because of what I could feel in'

[00:51] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Chris standing in a field, explaining the impact of subtle camera movements.
AUDIO: 'that moment. You\'d probably not notice it if you\'d watched it back, but it landed differently because of it. Now, if I said "And it was at that moment I knew my'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'life would never be the same again." versus without doing that zoom, "And it was at that moment I knew my life would never be the same again."'

[01:16] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris walking on a path, discussing shooting in 4K for editing flexibility.
AUDIO: 'That\'s the job, reading what\'s someone is actually saying and letting the frame respond to it and a phone on a tripod can\'t do any of that.'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It just sits there. Well, here\'s how I get it anyway. I shoot everything in 4K and I put everything out in HD, which means I\'ve got room. I can scale into a shot in the'

[01:41] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris walking on a path, explaining the limits of digital zoom.
AUDIO: 'edit and ease on a line that kind of matters. It\'s not identical to a real zoom or a real dolly towards you, but the background doesn\'t shift in the same way'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it would, but it gets you that kind of same feeling. And that\'s me being my own camera operator after the fact when I can actually see the shot, which I can\'t do when I\'m stood in it. Well, there\'s'

[02:06] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris walking on a path, demonstrating how to hide fluffed lines.
AUDIO: 'also a limit. I go to about 200% and no further. Past that, it starts to kind of fall apart. The other thing to mention here is'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you can\'t do it all the time. Save it for when the shot actually needs it rather than doing it over and over. But there is a bonus here, too. If I fluff a line, I can record it again, and'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the edit, I can jump in to a tighter version for a sentence or two, and then hop back out so the fluff disappears and it looks like the shot just changed.'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you didn\'t do this, there\'d be a jump cut like this. See? Feels jarring, right? So, why bother with any of it? Because the shot size should match how intense'

[02:59] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris walking on a path, demonstrating walking towards the camera to change shot size.
AUDIO: 'the thing you\'re saying is. Does that make sense? Think about sitting across from a mate. When they want to tell you a secret or things get intense, you end up closer together, don\'t you?'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, a big point wants to be tighter. Establishing a scene benefits from being wider. And when it\'s wrong, you feel it. Too tight for a light line, and it\'s a bit'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bananas. Too wide for an emotional one, and well, nothing lands. We can\'t really see your face. Watch any film where things get serious, the camera creeps in or it\'s closer. That\'s'

[03:37] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris walking on a path, combining walking and digital zoom.
AUDIO: 'all we\'re doing here. And here\'s a free one that costs nothing. You don\'t need any of the 4K stuff to change your shot size, either. You can just walk. Start further away from the camera and come'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'towards the camera at the point where things build. You\'re tightening the shot using your feet. And if you want to be flash about it, you can do both.'

[04:02] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Chris standing on a porch, discussing using an Apple Watch for monitoring.
AUDIO: 'Walk in and ease in at the same time. So, you arrive at the important line together. Right, there\'s a practical problem here.'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s the tighter you go, the harder it is to know where you are in frame. So, when I\'m shooting my short videos, I use my Apple Watch.'

[04:27] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Chris standing on a porch, explaining alternatives to an Apple Watch for monitoring.
AUDIO: 'It\'s got an app called camera control. It\'s always built in there. It gives me a live feed of the camera and I can set the frame from where I\'m actually standing.'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Before that, I was setting a mark, recording a bit of video, testing, watching it back, moving the mark, and starting again. Now, I just look at my wrist and check the mark, and done. But, if you haven\'t got an Apple Watch,'

[04:52] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Chris standing on a porch, demonstrating using physical objects for framing.
AUDIO: 'two options. A cheap wireless monitor, 60 or 70 quid, does the same job. There are some SmallRig ones, which my wife uses. They\'re not too bad. Or the free version, which I like better anyway, is'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build a frame like this and place in it where you obviously go. Like a bench, a railing to lean on, a gate. This spot is guaranteed to be framed okay if I just'

[05:19] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris standing in a field, discussing leaving room for graphics.
AUDIO: 'lean on this railing. Because then you\'re not guessing. You know you\'re in shot because that\'s the only place you can be.'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The other thing an operator does is to frame what\'s coming. So, I put myself on a third of the frame and leave the other side empty for text to slide into. Shooting vertically, I leave room up top'

[05:45] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris standing in a field, introducing the five essential shot sizes.
AUDIO: 'instead. And I can look slightly towards that graphic into that space, so I\'m almost looking at it when it arrives. If you frame yourself bang in the'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'middle, where\'s that text going? Over your face, probably. If you take one thing from this one video, take this bit. When shooting your content, you need five shots. You need a'

[06:11] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Chris in a close-up shot, demonstrating a close-up.
AUDIO: 'wide shot, which establishes where you are. A long shot, which is basically head to toe. A mid shot, which is from your waist up. That\'s your home'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'position. That\'s where you live. A mid close-up, which is from the chest up for when things matter. And a close-up, your face kind of fills the frame. Use it almost never. It\'s very'

[06:37] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris in a mid-shot, explaining the five shot sizes.
AUDIO: 'intimate, and we\'ve only just met. Get those five, and you can cut between any of them, and it just works. And here\'s'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where it goes wrong though, and I\'ve not heard many people mention this one. If you cut from a mid shot to another mid shot, it feels like you\'re being punched in the face. Nothing\'s changed, just'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything\'s jumped. The background might change, but you\'re still the same size. And in short form, in vertical videos, it\'s about your eyes. Watching on your phone is your phone\'s'

[07:14] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Chris in a mid-shot, discussing avoiding cuts between similar shot sizes.
AUDIO: 'this close. Your face is here. So, putting your eyes in a similar part of the frame in each shot really helps. Your video becomes much less tiring to watch, and ultimately, you\'ll get more'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'views because of it. Let me show you a quick short I made about it. Try this, because if you can keep your eyes in a similar part of the shot in each frame. And if you can do that'

[07:42] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Chris in a vertical video, demonstrating eye-line consistency.
AUDIO: 'consistently, and if you could do it across all shot sizes, it will look better than if my eyes are here and then down here and then up here and then back down here again. And because of that,'

[07:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'videos just become tiring to watch. And when they become tiring to watch, people just scroll on. But they might not be able to tell you why. So, nail your shot sizes, then start'

[08:08] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Chris sitting on a roller, summarizing the key takeaways.
AUDIO: 'adding in the rest because with great power comes great responsibility. I\'m not zooming in on every single shot. One move, maybe two moves in a long video is'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plenty. Subtle wins. We\'re doing everything deliberately, not just letting AI edit things together in a very random way. You see it online all the time. And if this was useful,'

[08:36] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Chris sitting on a roller, encouraging comments and subscriptions.
AUDIO: 'subscribe. One a week. And watch this one next. It\'s about moving on camera so you can stop feeling stiff. And it\'s the other half of this video. In the'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comments, what shot sizes are you filming everything in at the moment? Have a think. I bet it\'s kind of the same one every single time. I read every comment.'
